WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.508
Storage selection is an architectural argument about
the application, not a contest between database

00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:06.964
labels.

00:00:06.964 --> 00:00:10.609
Start with a small set of operations and their
promises.

00:00:10.609 --> 00:00:16.271
Then choose the least complicated arrangement that
can meet and operate those promises.

00:00:16.271 --> 00:00:22.389
A community workshop needs to reserve machines,
search project descriptions and serve tutorial

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:23.170
photographs.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.498
Reservations must not overlap for the same machine.

00:00:26.498 --> 00:00:32.576
Search can tolerate a short indexing delay, while
photographs are large immutable objects once

00:00:32.576 --> 00:00:33.223
published.

00:00:33.223 --> 00:00:39.172
These are three access patterns within one product;
the shared user interface does not imply

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.176
identical storage requirements.

00:00:41.176 --> 00:00:47.578
Begin with a relational reservation store and object
storage for photographs, then evaluate whether

00:00:47.578 --> 00:00:50.811
its existing text-search capability is sufficient.

00:00:50.811 --> 00:00:57.079
A separate search service becomes worthwhile only
when requirements justify its indexing pipeline

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:58.242
and recovery work.

00:00:58.242 --> 00:01:04.187
Record expected request rates, acceptable staleness,
atomic operations and who operates each

00:01:04.187 --> 00:01:04.833
component.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:10.907
A technically capable store may still be a poor
choice if the team cannot back it up, diagnose

00:01:10.907 --> 00:01:14.009
failures or restore it within the required time.

00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:20.033
A transaction groups changes, while its isolation
and update protocol determine how concurrent

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.186
requests interact.

00:01:21.186 --> 00:01:24.454
We can see the distinction with a single projector.

00:01:24.454 --> 00:01:30.861
Follow the successful path, the competing request,
and then the failure that occurs halfway through.

00:01:30.861 --> 00:01:33.231
A makerspace has one spare projector.

00:01:33.231 --> 00:01:38.293
Two requests each read available_count equal to one
and both attempt a booking.

00:01:38.311 --> 00:01:44.256
Wrapping each request’s statements in a transaction
gives all-or-nothing changes, but does not by

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:49.466
itself specify isolation strong enough to prevent
both from making the same decision.

00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:53.695
The read-and-update interaction needs deliberate
concurrency control.

00:01:53.695 --> 00:01:59.824
One approach conditionally decrements the count only
when it is positive, checks whether exactly one

00:01:59.824 --> 00:02:03.808
row changed, and creates the reservation in the same
transaction.

00:02:03.811 --> 00:02:10.239
The database’s supported atomic update semantics
must ensure competing updates are evaluated safely.

00:02:10.239 --> 00:02:14.995
The losing request reports unavailability rather
than inserting a booking.

00:02:14.995 --> 00:02:18.852
If creating the reservation fails, the decrement
rolls back.

00:02:18.852 --> 00:02:24.958
Retried requests also need a way to recognize an
already committed booking rather than silently

00:02:24.958 --> 00:02:26.373
creating a second one.

00:02:26.411 --> 00:02:32.626
One dataset can support both immediate operations
and broad analysis, but those workloads ask the

00:02:32.626 --> 00:02:34.548
hardware to do different work.

00:02:34.548 --> 00:02:40.443
Trace the columns required by a dashboard and then
compare its freshness needs with the live

00:02:40.443 --> 00:02:41.532
delivery service.

00:02:41.532 --> 00:02:47.107
A delivery dashboard computes average journey time
by district from 120 million events.

00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:53.338
It needs district, duration and date, but not the
driver’s notes or the full delivery address.

00:02:53.338 --> 00:02:59.763
A column-oriented representation can read a smaller
relevant subset and compress repeated values.

00:02:59.763 --> 00:03:06.321
The final result may contain only a few dozen rows,
so aggregation near storage avoids transferring

00:03:06.321 --> 00:03:08.176
the entire event collection.

00:03:08.211 --> 00:03:14.219
The operational service instead checks and updates
one delivery’s state repeatedly throughout the

00:03:14.219 --> 00:03:14.466
day.

00:03:14.466 --> 00:03:18.616
That is a different access pattern, even if both
stores accept SQL.

00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:24.748
If analytics imports events every fifteen minutes, a
dashboard can omit recent completed deliveries

00:03:24.748 --> 00:03:27.040
without either database being broken.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.862
Display the data’s freshness and use the operational
source for decisions that require current

00:03:32.862 --> 00:03:33.234
state.

00:03:33.234 --> 00:03:39.180
Benchmark representative queries rather than
assuming a storage layout makes every query faster.
